        {
          "order": 1,
          "label": "List",
          "type": "Text",
          "bbox_xyxy": [
            120.0,
            320.0,
            1179.5,
            1007.6
          ],
          "conf": 0.507,
          "text": "1. The notation $\\sum_{k=0}^{n} {}^{n}\\mathrm{C}_{k} a^{n-k} b^{k}$ stands for\n\n${}^{n}\\mathrm{C}_{0} a^{n} b^{0} + {}^{n}\\mathrm{C}_{1} a^{n-1} b^{1} + ... + {}^{n}\\mathrm{C}_{r} a^{n-r} b^{r} + ... + {}^{n}\\mathrm{C}_{n} a^{n-n} b^{n}$, where $b^{0} = 1 = a^{n-n}$.\nHence the theorem can also be stated as\n\n$$(a+b)^{n} = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} {}^{n}\\mathrm{C}_{k} a^{n-k} b^{k}.$$\n\n2. The coefficients ${}^{n}\\mathrm{C}_{r}$ occuring in the binomial theorem are known as binomial\ncoefficients.\n3. There are $(n+1)$ terms in the expansion of $(a+b)^{n}$, i.e., one more than the index.\n4. In the successive terms of the expansion the index of $a$ goes on decreasing by\nunity. It is $n$ in the first term, $(n-1)$ in the second term, and so on ending with zero\nin the last term. At the same time the index of $b$ increases by unity, starting with\nzero in the first term, 1 in the second and so on ending with $n$ in the last term.\n5. In the expansion of $(a+b)^{n}$, the sum of the indices of $a$ and $b$ is $n+0 = n$ in the\nfirst term, $(n-1) + 1 = n$ in the second term and so on $0+n = n$ in the last term.\nThus, it can be seen that the sum of the indices of $a$ and $b$ is $n$ in every term of the\nexpansion."
        },
        {
          "order": 2,
          "label": "Sub-section-title",
          "type": "SectionHeader",
          "bbox_xyxy": [
            119.2,
            1012.7,
            849.3,
            1064.2
          ],
          "conf": 0.859,
          "text": "7.2.2 Some special cases In the expansion of $(a + b)^n$,"
        },
        {
          "order": 3,
          "label": "List",
          "type": "Text",
          "bbox_xyxy": [
            120.1,
            1066.7,
            654.7,
            1118.2
          ],
          "conf": 0.738,
          "text": "(i) Taking $a = x$ and $b = -y$, we obtain"
        },
        {
          "order": 4,
          "label": "Equation",
          "type": "Equation",
          "bbox_xyxy": [
            186.8,
            1117.6,
            1176.9,
            1277.6
          ],
          "conf": 0.821,
          "text": "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x-y)^n &= [x + (\\rightarrow y)]^n \\\\\n&= {}^nC_0x^n + {}^nC_1x^{n-1}(-y) + {}^nC_2x^{n-2}(-y)^2 + {}^nC_3x^{n-3}(-y)^3 + ... + {}^nC_n(-y)^n \\\\\n&= {}^nC_0x^n - {}^nC_1x^{n-1}y + {}^nC_2x^{n-2}y^2 - {}^nC_3x^{n-3}y^3 + ... + (-1)^n {}^nC_n y^n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
        {
          "order": 5,
          "label": "Paragraph",
          "type": "Text",
          "bbox_xyxy": [
            120.4,
            1276.6,
            939.1,
            1333.8
          ],
          "conf": 0.739,
          "text": "Thus $(x-y)^n = {}^n\\mathrm{C}_0 x^n - {}^n\\mathrm{C}_1 x^{n-1} y + {}^n\\mathrm{C}_2 x^{n-2} y^2 + ... + (-1)^n {}^n\\mathrm{C}_n y^n$"
        },
        {
          "order": 6,
          "label": "Equation",
          "type": "Equation",
          "bbox_xyxy": [
            121.8,
            1330.0,
            1178.5,
            1486.0
          ],
          "conf": 0.812,
          "text": "Using this, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x-2y)^5 &= {}^5C_0x^5 - {}^5C_1x^4(2y) + {}^5C_2x^3(2y)^2 - {}^5C_3x^2(2y)^3 + \\\\\n&= {}^5C_4x(2y)^4 - {}^5C_5(2y)^5 \\\\\n&= x^5 - 10x^4y + 40x^3y^2 - 80x^2y^3 + 80xy^4 - 32y^5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
